    "title" "Mod Profiler (dev tool)"
    "description" "[h1]Mod Profiler[/h1]
A tool for mod developers.
This is for people writing mods, not for playing. It costs nothing while it is closed.

This mod adds a 'Mod Profiler' page to the top bar of the game next to the 'Statistics' button.
Click it to open up a debugging panel where you can view a ton of debugging information about your (or other people's) mod!

[h2]Overview[/h2]
This page shows a general overview about how long frame draws are taking.
This lets you find out where frame times are going and determine if the render pipeline is the cause of slowness or lags.

[h2]Memory[/h2]
This panel lets you find out what objects are taking up RAM (what is on the heap).
Scan every C# object that is reachable in the game and groups the results by mod, so you can find which mods are being memory hogs, and find specifically which objects are taking up the most memory.
This can help you, or other mod creators find memory leaks.

[h2]CPU[/h2]
This panel lets you choose a mod, and press a 'Record' button.
After the selected amount of time has passed, a flame graph is shown for the mod's own methods with a selectable drill-down list beneath it.
 Click a frame to zoom into it, and hover to get more details. This helps show which functions and method calls are taking the most amount of time.

Recording weaves timing hooks into the chosen assembly and takes them out again on stop, so it is never on by default. It also stops itself - thirty seconds, two minutes, five, or never.
Be aware if you click never. If you start recording and do not stop the recording, you might end up with a massive flame graph in memory, which can infinitely eat your RAM.

While recording, every thread is recorded. The game simulation does not run on the main thread, so a mod's registered simulations happen on the thread pool.

[h2]Export[/h2]
Once you have analyzed the heap, or recorded a flame graph, you can export the data to send to a mod's creator (or to analyze yourself outside of the game).
Clicking the 'Export' button will generate a file in the mod folder alongside the mod. It will also copy the filepath where the file got created to your clipboard.
After clicking 'Export', simply open File Explorer and paste the copied path to find the created file.
The file gets created besides the Player.log.

[h2]Using it[/h2]
[list]
[*]Open it from the button beside Statistics in the top bar, or drive it from the debug console with [b]prof.[/b] commands (F1).
[*]Escape closes it. Nothing is written to your save, so you can add or remove it at any time.
[*]Requires Shapez Shifter and MonoMod.RuntimeDetour.
[/list]

Source and readme: https://github.com/QuinnBast/Shapez2-Mods/tree/main/Shapez2-Mod-Profiler
Bugs and requests: https://github.com/QuinnBast/Shapez2-Mods/issues
Licensed under Apache 2.0."
}
